  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 312 AM EST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nOn Wednesday evening, a low pressure system will be positioned over \nthe western Great Lakes which will extend a cold front southward to \nthe Georgia/Alabama state line. Numerous showers and scattered \nthunderstorms will be ongoing in the region of forcing ahead of the \nfront as the long term period begins. As the parent low pressure \nsystem occludes, the progression of the cold front will slow down as \nit slowly works its way southeast through the forecast area on \nThursday. Forcing ahead of the front will also weaken, with \nprecipitation becoming more scattered in nature over the course of \nthe day. Widespread severe weather is not expected at this time with \nstronger dynamics associated with this system likely to be displaced \nwell north of the forecast area. Rainfall amounts are currently \nforecast to be between 1-2 inches in north Georgia and 0.5-1 inch in \ncentral Georgia. With dewpoints in the mid to upper 60s and \nprecipitable water values between 1.6-1.8 inches, thunderstorms that \noccur will be capable of producing brief periods of heavy rain and \nlocally higher rainfall totals.\n\nThe frontal boundary is expected to clear the forecast area to the \nsoutheast by Thursday night. Initially there will be little sensible \nchange in the airmass, with relatively warm and humid conditions \nremaining underneath southwesterly flow aloft. Low temperatures on \nFriday morning will be in the low 50s in areas north of I-85 and \nin the mid 50s to low 60s in areas along and south of I-85. High \ntemperatures will rise into the 70s across the majority of the area \non Friday afternoon, with low 80s in east-central Georgia. A weaker \ncold front and isolated to widely scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will move southward through the area on Friday \nafternoon and evening on Friday. Cooler conditions with temperatures \ncloser to daily averages will return this weekend behind this second \nfront.",
